#             "explanation": "Understanding *Pedagogy of the Oppressed* requires examining its historical background in mid-twentieth-century Latin America. Freire developed his educational philosophy while teaching adult literacy to impoverished peasants in northeastern Brazil. He observed that traditional literacy programs treated marginalized learners as passive recipients, reinforcing existing social hierarchies.\n\nEpistemologically, Freire synthesizes elements of Marxist dialectics, Frantz Fanon's anti-colonial critique, Hegelian philosophy, and Christian humanism. From Hegel, Freire adopts the master-slave dialectic to explain how oppressed individuals internalize the consciousness of their oppressors. From Marxist theory, he draws on the analysis of class struggle, viewing education as an inherently political activity that either supports or challenges existing power structures.\n\nThis historical and philosophical framework explains why Freire rejects neutral pedagogy. He maintains that all educational practices carry political implications—either socialized acceptance of the status quo or critical empowerment that equips learners to transform their social realities."
